   "source": "prose written by Claude Sonnet 5.5 for this tool, 2026-10-20",
   "text": "A harbour town keeps its own time. In the early morning the fishing boats come in with the tide, and the men on the quay stack grey crates of mackerel and herring beside the ice house. By seven the bakers have opened their shutters, and the smell of warm bread drifts down the hill past the church, the post office and the little school where the children line up in pairs. Gulls wheel overhead and quarrel over scraps. An old woman sweeps the steps of the chandlery, as she has done every day for forty years, and nods to each person who passes.\n\nThe harbour master works in a narrow room above the customs shed. On his desk lie a brass barometer, a ledger of arrivals and a mug of tea that has gone cold. He writes down each vessel by name, by tonnage and by the hour of its mooring. When the weather turns, he rings the bell on the pier, and the boats still at sea make for the shelter of the breakwater. In winter the storms can last for days. The waves climb the sea wall and break over the road, and the town waits, with its lamps lit and its doors barred, until the wind drops.",
